From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yi Xie <imxieyi@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 15:38:53 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] um: let another CPU dispatch Darwin host events when CPU0 is
 late

Darwin has no per-thread SIGIO, so CPU0 alone collects the host's
descriptor events: at its clock tick, when it leaves idle, and when the
notification thread signals it while it idles. A guest whose CPU0 is
busy sees an event only at the next tick, and the host can keep that
carrier from running for hundreds of milliseconds under load, so a
descriptor that is ready waits for as long as the host holds CPU0 while
other CPUs run.

Let any CPU dispatch, one at a time. triggered[] and the IRQ core's
per-IRQ active and pending flags belong to whoever dispatches, so a
token guards them: a compare-and-swap that never waits, taken around
every call of sigio_handler(). CPU0 tries at every tick and idle exit,
as before. Any other CPU tries only when no CPU has begun a dispatch for
two ticks (20 ms), at its own tick and at its idle exit. A CPU that
finds the token taken skips, and the edge stays in the dispatch queue
for the holder's loop or the next dispatch; each EV_CLEAR edge is
returned by exactly one kevent(), and the token orders the dispatchers.
Generic irq.c is unchanged.

The notification thread never waits for CPU0 any more, so it reads the
next edge while CPU0 is held. For an edge that no dispatch has begun
after within 20 ms, whether CPU0 is busy or idle and not running, it
writes a byte to the wake pipe of a secondary that is idle, which
dispatches from its idle exit; no signal is sent, so nothing interrupts
a CPU in guest code. It asks again every 20 ms until a dispatch begins,
sooner while no secondary is idle. The wake it owes a CPU0 that is not
idle is left to that CPU0's next idle entry instead of a parked thread.

CPU0's drain before an idle wait now sleeps for at most 200 us when
another CPU held the token, so that an edge that arrived meanwhile is
not slept on.

A system suspend runs with the secondaries offline, so it is unchanged.
A healthy CPU0 dispatches within a tick, so nothing is taken over.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
